      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <p class="kicker">One dataset, several desks</p>
        <h2>One dataset, more than one buyer.</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="reveal">A support archive, a document set and a deal history are worth different amounts to different AI teams. Rather than taking the first offer, we put the opportunity in front of the buyers we work with and compare what comes back on price, scope and term. A buyer that passes on one category often wants another.</p>
      <p class="reveal">You still approve every buyer before anything is shared, and each agreement is between your company and the buyer you accept.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <p class="kicker">Before you sign anything</p>
        <h2>Eight questions to ask any buyer. Including ours.</h2>
        <p>These are the terms that decide what a deal is worth and what you are left able to do afterwards. Ask every buyer the same questions, and ask for the answers in writing.</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>What is the exact employee floor for my sector?</strong>The floors differ between programs, and between pages. Get the number that applies to you.</li>
        <li><strong>Is the license exclusive, and for how long?</strong>Exclusivity can outlast the payment and bar you from licensing the same records elsewhere.</li>
        <li><strong>When is the raw upload deleted?</strong>A date, not a policy.</li>
        <li><strong>Can I audit a sample of the de-identified copy?</strong>You should see what the buyer sees before it is delivered.</li>
        <li><strong>Which categories are excluded?</strong>Health records, payment-card data and children&rsquo;s data are commonly carved out.</li>
        <li><strong>Who is my counterparty, and who pays whom?</strong>One agreement, one entity that owes you money.</li>
        <li><strong>What triggers payment, and when does it land?</strong>Acceptance, invoice and delivery are three different dates.</li>
        <li><strong>Can I see the offer in writing before I commit?</strong>A nonbinding letter of intent is standard, and costs you nothing.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="reveal">A broker that cannot answer these before you sign has told you something useful. Our answers, where the buyer publishes them, are on <a href="what-affects-a-deal.html">what affects a deal</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">What it does not do</h2>
      <p class="reveal">The introduction changes who carries the conversation, not what you agree to.</p>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>Skip the rights review</strong>Every record set is checked before a buyer sees it, and the scope is set in writing first.</li>
        <li><strong>Share anything on your behalf</strong>Nothing leaves your systems until you have approved the terms.</li>
        <li><strong>Decide for you</strong>You approve every step, and you can stop at any point.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="link-row reveal"><a href="trust.html">How we handle your data</a> <a href="what-affects-a-deal.html">What affects a deal</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">Why an AI deal is not a normal data license</h2>
      <p class="reveal">A standard license names a purpose and stops. The records here can spawn new material, so the terms have to say what happens at each layer: cleaned datasets, annotations, embeddings, evaluation sets, fine-tuned models, model weights and outputs.</p>
      <p class="reveal">That work belongs to the buyers rather than to you, but it explains why the conversation is specific about purpose and scope long before anyone discusses a price.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

